WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.533
Now the real job: start from one measurement and find all six elements.

00:00:04.533 --> 00:00:06.640
We'll use the textbook's example.

00:00:06.967 --> 00:00:08.598
First, the lengths.

00:00:08.598 --> 00:00:14.520
The position vector is ten thousand six hundred nine kilometers long.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:19.927
The speed is seven point seven five five kilometers per second.

00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:23.634
The energy is v squared over two, minus mu over r.

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:28.954
That's minus seven point five zero three kilometers squared per second squared.

00:00:28.954 --> 00:00:30.907
Negative, so it's an ellipse.

00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:38.086
Energy gives the semi major axis directly: a equals minus mu over two E.

00:00:38.086 --> 00:00:43.527
That's twenty six thousand five hundred sixty four kilometers.

00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:49.713
That's about four Earth radii, and a period of almost exactly twelve hours.

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:55.713
Next, the angular momentum: h equals r cross v.

00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:14.317
h sticks straight out of the orbit plane, so it already pins down the plane's tilt.

00:01:14.317 --> 00:01:16.133
We'll use that in a moment.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:24.300
Now the eccentricity vector, from the last chapter: v cross h over mu, minus the unit vector along r.

00:01:28.166 --> 00:01:31.940
Its length is the eccentricity: zero point seven four one one.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:35.166
And it points from the focus straight to the perigee.

00:01:35.566 --> 00:01:45.716
Your notes give a second way to get the same number, from energy and h alone: e equals the square root of one plus two E h squared over mu squared.

00:01:45.716 --> 00:01:46.406
It agrees.

00:01:46.766 --> 00:01:59.766
So the perigee radius is about six thousand eight hundred seventy seven kilometers, just five hundred kilometers up, and the apogee is about forty six thousand two hundred fifty kilometers away.

00:02:01.199 --> 00:02:05.263
Now build the perifocal frame from these two vectors.

00:02:05.263 --> 00:02:09.479
p hat is the eccentricity vector divided by its length.

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:14.319
h hat is h divided by its length.

00:02:17.232 --> 00:02:22.592
And q hat completes the right handed set: h hat cross p hat.

00:02:25.866 --> 00:02:30.866
Stack them as rows, and that's C P N, with real numbers in it.

00:02:31.199 --> 00:02:34.851
The corner entry is zero point four four seven eight.

00:02:34.851 --> 00:02:39.399
Its arc cosine is the inclination: sixty three point four degrees.

00:02:40.866 --> 00:02:45.065
For capital Omega, take the first two entries of the bottom row.

00:02:45.065 --> 00:02:47.952
C three one is zero point five one two nine.

00:02:47.952 --> 00:02:53.266
C three two is zero point seven three two four, so minus C three two is negative.

00:02:53.599 --> 00:02:55.829
Positive sine, negative cosine.

00:02:55.829 --> 00:02:57.771
That's the second quadrant.

00:02:57.771 --> 00:03:01.079
atan two gives one hundred forty five degrees.

00:03:01.433 --> 00:03:06.926
The textbook gets the same answer with the node vector: n equals K cross h.

00:03:06.926 --> 00:03:11.393
It points along the line of nodes, toward the ascending node.

00:03:11.733 --> 00:03:21.772
Its components give cosine Omega equals minus zero point eight one nine two, and sine Omega equals zero point five seven three six.

00:03:21.772 --> 00:03:26.293
The same quadrant, the same one hundred forty five degrees.

00:03:26.699 --> 00:03:31.035
A fun coincidence: q hat equals the node direction here.

00:03:31.035 --> 00:03:37.539
That's not an accident; it happens because little omega turns out to be two seventy.

00:03:38.999 --> 00:03:49.559
Little omega uses the third column: C one three is minus zero point eight nine four two, and C two three is zero.

00:03:49.966 --> 00:03:54.498
atan two of a negative number and zero is minus ninety degrees.

00:03:54.498 --> 00:03:58.886
Add three sixty: little omega is two hundred seventy degrees.

00:03:59.232 --> 00:04:06.753
The textbook route: the cosine of omega is n dot e over n e, which is essentially zero.

00:04:06.753 --> 00:04:09.692
So omega is ninety or two seventy.

00:04:09.692 --> 00:04:12.112
Arc cosine can't tell which.

00:04:12.532 --> 00:04:17.601
Look at the eccentricity vector's K component: minus zero point six six two seven.

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:20.692
Perigee is below the equator, so it's two seventy.

00:04:21.066 --> 00:04:27.786
That matches the picture: perigee at the very bottom of the orbit, apogee high over the north.

00:04:29.232 --> 00:04:31.122
Last, the true anomaly.

00:04:31.122 --> 00:04:38.352
Its cosine is e dot r over e times r, which comes out to zero point one seven three six.

00:04:38.732 --> 00:04:40.479
Arc cosine says eighty degrees.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:45.212
But the satellite could be eighty degrees past perigee, or eighty degrees before it.

00:04:45.565 --> 00:04:46.965
Which one is it?

00:04:50.632 --> 00:04:51.818
Check r dot v.

00:04:51.818 --> 00:04:56.643
It's minus forty four thousand six hundred seventy three.

00:04:56.643 --> 00:05:03.586
Negative means the distance is shrinking: the satellite is falling toward perigee.

00:05:03.586 --> 00:05:09.512
So theta zero is three sixty minus eighty: two hundred eighty degrees.

00:05:10.532 --> 00:05:19.652
And that's all six: a, e, i, capital Omega, little omega, and theta zero, from one measurement.
